+**Method (Source [S2, unofficial] - Direct Formula):**
+This source provides a simpler, branch-free formula that assumes a specific orientation (flat-topped or pointy-topped depending on interpretation, but effectively covering the hexagon via max operations).
+1.  Compute absolute value: $q = |p|$.
+2.  Calculate cross-section distance $d_{xy}$:
+    $$d_{xy} = \max(q_x \cdot \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} + q_y \cdot \frac{1}{2}, q_y) - h_x$$
+    *Note*: The constant $0.866025 \approx \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}$. This formula effectively computes the distance to the closest of the 6 half-planes defining the hexagon.
+3.  Calculate cap distance $d_z = q_z - h_y$.
+4.  Combine:
+    $$d = \max(d_z, d_{xy})$$
