 ## Background
-The Hexagonal Prism SDF is part of the family of polyhedral primitives used in ray marching and constructive solid geometry (CSG) [S1, S2, unofficial]. [S1, unofficial] introduces the reflection/folding technique to exploit hexagonal symmetry, reducing the distance calculation to a fundamental sector. [S2, unofficial] provides a direct max-based formulation optimized for real-time GLSL shaders. Both are widely used in procedural modeling, real-time graphics, and collision detection [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Signed distance functions for polyhedra are widely used in ray marching for implicit modeling. The hexagonal prism SDF is a specific case of extruded polygons. The folding technique leverages the rotational symmetry of the hexagon to reduce the computational domain.
 
